 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490165 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490165 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490165 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490165 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490165 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490165 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490165 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9c2858be-b520-5ff3-a27a-b17e0ad5c144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490171 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490171 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490171 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490171 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490171 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490171 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b76bb8b2-2c1f-5117-8531-fe1007b02a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490183 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490183 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490183 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490183 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490183 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490183 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490183 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490183 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d061760f-e203-56c1-ab82-137a1fefdf2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490192 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490192 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490192 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490192 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490192 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490192 #edb/ref 1077 "field-5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490192 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490192 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dfadd8cf-a2e0-5f5b-a357-b9ae83b9b1c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490216 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490216 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the infimum of the set $C=\\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}?$\n\n$inf(C)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ecf28e6-85ea-58f8-8358-bdfe911fb50e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490216 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of a set $C$ is the greatest lower bound of $C.$\n\nIf $C$ is a finite set, the infimum of $C$ equals its minimum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(C) & =min\\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490221 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490221 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490221 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490221 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{5-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490221 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2472238f-de89-5651-9808-c29025aa1081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490221 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/1e/1ea2ed214496ed38ae605e9ac64bebd781a0d5529561fce44d98cca6839d6fbb.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\ge 5$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x<5$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $5-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$ is greater than $x$ for any $x<5.$\n\nTherefore, $[5,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[5,\\infty )=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that $5$ is *not* the maximum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no maximum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=5\\notin A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490234 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490234 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490234 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490234 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot n:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490234 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "332a6eec-904f-5523-bf1a-c62328e3b046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490234 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{1+1}\\cdot 1 & =1 \\\\ (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot 2 & =-2 \\\\ (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot 3 & =3 \\\\ (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot 4 & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490259 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490259 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490259 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A=[1,\\sqrt{5}].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(A).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490259 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64fea66a-24ac-5ed4-9315-868a66adf40d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490259 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{5}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{5}$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min\\,[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{5}$ is also the maximum of $A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $A$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(A)=\\sqrt{5}\\in A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A=(-\\infty ,-1).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(A)$ and $sup(A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(A)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(A)$ {{field-6}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fdea743-d7bb-5faa-abc9-1bdf19e0dbae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490270 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $A$ is unbounded below, $A$ has no lower bounds, and $inf(A)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge -1$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[-1,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<-1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(A)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(A)=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
